kosha civil-varga

  -- cluster for civil engineering: structures that shape space.
  -- civil engineering is the oldest engineering discipline —
  -- the pyramids, the Parthenon, the Roman aqueducts, the Eiffel Tower.
  -- all of them ask the same question: will gravity be resisted?
  --
  -- the fundamental problem of civil engineering:
  --   gravity pulls everything downward. structures must resist this.
  --   the structure must transfer all loads safely to the ground.
  --   the ground is the ultimate support — everything rests on it.
  --
  -- three types of internal force in a structure:
  --   compression: material is being squeezed. concrete, stone, masonry are strong.
  --                arch and dome — all compression, no tension.
  --   tension:     material is being pulled apart. steel, rope, cable are strong.
  --                suspension bridge — all cables in tension.
  --   shear:       material is being slid sideways. failure at joints, welds.
  --
  -- the key quantities:
  --   stress:   force per unit area. σ = F/A.  (pascals)
  --   strain:   fractional deformation. ε = ΔL/L. (dimensionless)
  --   Young's modulus: E = σ/ε. stiffness of material.
  --   yield strength: stress at which permanent deformation begins.
  --   ultimate strength: stress at fracture.
  --   safety factor: ultimate strength / working stress. must be > 1 (typically 3-10).
  --
  -- materials:
  --   concrete: strong in compression (30-50 MPa), weak in tension (3 MPa).
  --   steel:    strong in both (yield: 250-700 MPa).
  --   stone:    strong in compression only. no tensile strength.
  --   reinforced concrete: concrete + steel rebar — strong in both.

  "engineering-varga-vishesa"
  "subanta-swarupa"

  "gravity-yukta"               -- the fundamental load
  "normal-force-yukta"          -- structural resistance
  "compression-yukta tension-yukta shear-yukta"
  "stress-yukta strain-yukta"
  "concrete-yukta steel-yukta stone-yukta"
  "safety-factor-yukta"

